  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 1259 AM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nCarbon copies of Monday and Tuesday look to occur on Wednesday and \nsomewhat on Thursday.  At least from the heat standpoint on Thursday \nwe should be similar.  In fact Thursday could turn out to be the \nhottest day best on the setup, but we are looking at that high \npressure being far enough east that now the return south to \nsouthwest flow will now start to bring both Gulf and Pacific \nmoisture over the Panhandles and increase those chances of showers \nand storms, but mainly for the northwest Panhandles.  Thursday \nafternoon we may have an interesting setup.  Models are suggesting \nthat we may have residual outflow from storms off the mountains on \nWednesday track through the central Panhandles and collide with \nmoist southeast flow during peak heating, which given the hot \ntemperatures and the strong low level convergence that we could get \ngood storm development across the HWY 60 corridor and areas to the \nnorth.  Still several days out and a lot can change, but moving \nforward the overall setup of the Panhandles being more on the back \nside of the upper ridge, and more moist surface flow as well as \nsouth to southwest flow aloft would support better thunderstorm \nchances through the weekend.  It may even lead to some increased \nsevere chances, or possible hydro concerns given that storms may be \nslow movers. Will remind that while precipitation chances will be up \nand temperatures will be down from our current state, overall the \nbest chances for meaningful rainfall would be the northwest, and \nanything south and east of HWY 60 is highly in question as to how \nmuch or even if rain will occur at all. \n\nWeber",
